<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abtai Sain Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1554–1588</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Abtai Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abtai Sain Khan was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> Mongol prince, a great-grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a>, who became the leading ruler of central Khalkha in the late 16th century. A champion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdene-zuu">Erdene Zuu</a> beside the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> around 1585, and in 1586 he met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Third Dalai Lama</a>, Sonam Gyatso, who gave him the title of khan. His descendants ruled as the Tüsheet Khans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abtai-sain-khan.jpg" alt="Abtai Sain Khan" width="255" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AbtaiPainting_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
